.\" Public Domain.  After rx.4, for the diskettes of a PC.
.\"
.\"	@(#)fl.4	1.0 (2.11BSD) 2026/11/22
.\"
.TH FL 4 "November 22, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
fl \- PC diskettes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
.nf
major device number(s):
	raw: 11
	block: 3
minor device encoding:
	bits 0070 specify drive: <fl_drive>
	bit 01 specifies density: double: 0, high: 1
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I fl
device provides access to the two diskette drives of a PC/AT's
diskette controller (an NEC 765 at port 0x3F0, with interrupt request 6
and DMA channel 2).
A drive is 3.5-inch or 5.25-inch, as the machine's setup program
recorded it in the clock chip's memory.
Diskettes are double-sided with 80 cylinders and sectors of 512 bytes.
.PP
The standard device names begin with ``fl'' followed by
the drive number and then a letter a-b for double and high density
access to the drive respectively.
The character ? stands here for a drive number in the range 0-1.
.PP
The block files access the diskette via the system's normal
buffering mechanism and may be read and written without regard to
physical disk records.  There is also a `raw' interface
which provides for direct transmission between the diskette
and the user's read or write buffer.
The names of the raw files
conventionally begin with an extra `r.'
In raw I/O counts and
.I seek
offsets must be a multiple of 512 bytes.
.PP
In addition to normal (`block' and `raw') i/o, the driver supports
formatting of diskettes for either density.
.SH "DISK SUPPORT"
The
.I fl
driver does not support pseudo-disks (partitions) and a diskette has
no label.  Each file
.I fl?[ab]
refers to the entire diskette as a single sequentially addressed
file.
.PP
The size and density of the diskettes are specified as follows:
.PP
.nf
.ta .5i +\w'fl0b  'u +\w'3.5-inch high density  'u +\w'sectors/track  'u +\w'sectors  'u
	disk	in a drive that is	sectors/track	sectors	disktab
	fl?a	3.5-inch, either density	9	1440	fl720
	fl?b	3.5-inch high density	18	2880	fl1440
	fl?b	5.25-inch high density	15	2400	fl1200
.DT
.fi
.PP
A 5.25-inch drive has no
.I fl?a
and a 3.5-inch double density drive no
.IR fl?b .
Diskettes of 360 kilobytes are not supported.
.PP
The commands
.nf
.RS
% flformat /dev/rfl0b
% newfs \-T fl1440 /dev/rfl0b
.RE
.fi
format and make a file system on the high density diskette in a
3.5-inch drive 0.
.IR Tar (1)
and
.IR dump (8)
may write to the raw file directly.
.PP
A diskette with a file system that has
.I /boot
and a kernel in its root directory, and the boot block in its first
sector, starts the system:
.nf
.RS
% dd if=/mdec/fluboot of=/dev/rfl0b count=1
.RE
.fi
The kernel so loaded takes its root file system from a hard disk, a
diskette having no room for a root file system and a swap area.
.PP
An
.IR ioctl (2)
call is available to format a diskette:
.RS
.nf
.ft B
#include <i286at/flreg.h>
ioctl(fildes, FLIOC_FORMAT)
.ft R
.fi
.RE
The density is determined by the device opened.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev/rfl[01][ab]  'u
/dev/fl[01][ab]	block files
.br
/dev/rfl[01][ab]	raw files
.br
/mdec/fluboot	boot block
.DT
.SH "SEE ALSO"
rx(4),
disktab(5),
flformat(8),
newfs(8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
\fBfl%d%c: hard error sn%d (sector %d) st0=%b st1=%b\fR.  An unrecoverable
error occurred during transfer of the specified sector of the specified
diskette.  The contents of the controller's first two status registers
are also printed symbolically with bits decoded.
The transfer was tried five times.
.PP
\fBfl%d: not ready\fR.  The controller did not answer within three
seconds, usually because no diskette is in the drive.
.SH BUGS
Formatting does not read back what it wrote.
.PP
A diskette changed while its file system is mounted is not noticed.
